The community garden meets this week. You know someone wrote down when
the gate opens, but your laptop contains a season of notes and none of
the filenames contains the word ``gate.'' The command you want is not
one that finds files \emph{named} like a gate. It is one that reads
selected files and shows the lines that say something about a gate.

\begin{objectives}
\item Search notes for literal words and phrases without editing the files.
\item Choose between a fixed string, a basic regular expression and an
      extended regular expression.
\item Tell the difference between a match, an empty result and a failed search.
\end{objectives}
\IndexCommand{grep}
\IndexTerm{regular expression}

\section{Start with the words you remember}
\label{sec:19-literal}
Use a disposable directory for all the examples. If you use Fish or
the C shell family, enter \cmd|sh| before the setup; the listings use
portable shell syntax. Keep this shell open until cleanup. Run the
lines separately, and stop if the first or second line fails: none
of the paths below should ever resolve inside a real notes folder.
The first line stores the directory name returned by \cmd|mktemp|;
\cmd|-d| asks for a directory rather than a file. The template's six
Xs stand for a generated suffix.

\begin{terminal}
practice=$(mktemp -d /tmp/utp19.XXXXXX)
cd "$practice"
printf '%s\n' 'Gate opens at 9' 'Bring gloves' \
  'Update: north gate opens at 10' \
  'Reminder: tea at 11' > meeting.txt
printf '%s\n' 'gate code changed' 'Bring tea bags' \
  'Buy stakes [urgent]' 'Repaint gatepost' > errands.txt
\end{terminal}

The backslash at the end of a physical line makes it continue on
the next one; it is not a backslash to insert in the note. The
\cmd|printf| format writes one argument per line. Use this fixture
rather than a private diary, a file of passwords, or a directory
that includes cached messages. A search can put information on
screen that you did not remember storing.

\begin{terminal}
grep -n -F 'gate opens' meeting.txt
\end{terminal}
\begin{terminaloutput}
3:Update: north gate opens at 10
\end{terminaloutput}

Put the options first, the pattern next, and the filenames last.
\cmd|-F| means fixed strings: the pattern is searched for as written.
\cmd|-n| prefixes each selected line with its number in the file;
the first line is 1. The number and colon are a label on the
output, not characters that \cmd|grep| added to the file. A match
need not occupy the whole line; the result contains the entire
line regardless of where the phrase occurs. The single quotes tell
the shell to pass the space as part of one argument. They are not
characters that must be present in the notes.

The result has missed the first line, which starts with uppercase
\texttt{G}. If you don't remember how something was capitalized,
add \cmd|-i| to ignore case when comparing characters. Each
filename after the pattern is another input. With more than one
input file, the output includes the filename before each line
number. A one-file search isn't missing information just because
it omits that prefix.

\begin{terminal}
grep -n -i -F 'gate' meeting.txt errands.txt
\end{terminal}
\begin{terminaloutput}
meeting.txt:1:Gate opens at 9
meeting.txt:3:Update: north gate opens at 10
errands.txt:1:gate code changed
errands.txt:4:Repaint gatepost
\end{terminaloutput}

\begin{figure}[htbp]
\centering
\input{figures/chapter-19/search.tikz}
\caption{Selecting a line is not the same as extracting the matching phrase.}
\label{fig:19-search}
\VisualDescription{The pattern is compared with each input line; both matching lines leave the command intact, and the unselected line does not.}
\end{figure}

The fourth result may look like a mistake if you meant the noun
``gate,'' not the label ``gatepost.'' The command can't infer
that distinction. It found the character sequence you specified.
Before changing the search, identify which result you would keep
and which you would throw out by hand. The difference suggests
what to tell the utility next. For example, if you care about
meeting times rather than equipment, refine back to the longer
phrase. If you are looking for any mention of the noun, including
next to a comma or at the end of a line, neither a longer phrase
nor adding a single space is a general solution.

\section{Specify a shape instead of a phrase}
\label{sec:19-regex}
Suppose the notes mix reminders and updates and you care only about
lines labeled as updates. Searching for \texttt{Update:} anywhere
would also accept a quotation containing that phrase. A
\FirstUse{regular expression} lets you say where a match belongs
as well as what characters it contains. With neither \cmd|-F| nor
\cmd|-E|, \cmd|grep| interprets its pattern as a basic regular
expression, often abbreviated BRE. Start with this one:

\begin{terminal}
grep -n '^Update:' meeting.txt
\end{terminal}

\noindent
You should see only line 3. The caret at the start is an
\FirstUse{anchor}: it tests the start of the line rather than
matching a printed caret. A dollar sign at the end of a pattern
tests the end of the line. The following search selects lines
that end with a digit. The two square brackets enclose a
one-character choice. The inner \texttt{[:digit:]} is the name of
a standard character class, not a second pattern of its own.

\begin{terminal}
grep -n '[[:digit:]]$' meeting.txt
\end{terminal}

This matches lines 1, 3 and 4. It is not a check that there is a
valid time: an arbitrary note ending with the digit 7 also matches.
If your editor leaves trailing spaces, a line that looks like it
ends in a digit may fail this test. A match is evidence that a
specific pattern succeeded, not that an interpretation is true.
Notice the distinction between an anchored expression and the
\cmd|-x| option, which requires the whole line to match the chosen
pattern. With \cmd|-F -x| the whole line must equal the literal
phrase; that combination requires no regular expression at all.

\begin{quicktable}{M{1.31in}Y}
\toprule
Pattern & Meaning in BRE and ERE here \\
\midrule
\texttt{\textasciicircum Update:} & The first seven characters must be the label \texttt{Update:}. \\
\texttt{[[:digit:]]\$} & There must be a digit immediately before the end of the line. \\
. & One character, not necessarily a period. Under \texttt{-F} it is a period. \\
ba* & The letter \texttt{b}, followed by zero or more occurrences of \texttt{a}. \\
\bottomrule
\end{quicktable}

The star is especially deceptive. In a shell glob (Chapter~\ref{ch:14})
a star can stand for a stretch of filename by itself. In a
regular expression it repeats the preceding expression. To match
a stretch of arbitrary characters in a regular expression, put it
after the dot: \cmd|.*|. Unless you need the location or shape of
the text around a phrase, you don't need to add either symbol:
\cmd|grep| already looks for the phrase within each line. Resist
the urge to add syntax just because you saw it in another search.

\begin{figure}[htbp]
\centering
\input{figures/chapter-19/parsers.tikz}
\caption{Quotes protect the pattern; the option selects who interprets it.}
\label{fig:19-parsers}
\VisualDescription{The shell passes the quoted bracket expression as one argument. Grep receives the brackets and treats them literally with its fixed-string option.}
\end{figure}

\begin{terminal}
grep -n -F '[urgent]' errands.txt
grep -n '[urgent]' errands.txt
\end{terminal}

The first command finds only the line about stakes. The second
finds all four lines, each of which contains at least one of the
letters between the brackets. This is a useful diagnostic:
switch to \cmd|-F| as soon as a search has surprising extra
matches and you had intended literal punctuation. Quoting alone
protects text from the shell, not from the utility's pattern
language (Figure~\ref{fig:19-parsers}).

\section{Choose a pattern language deliberately}
Extended regular expressions, abbreviated ERE, make several
common choices easier to write. Use \cmd|-E| for this grammar.
The vertical bar inside the quoted argument means ``or'' to
\cmd|grep|, not a pipeline to the shell. Parentheses group the
alternatives so that the anchor applies to both of them.

\begin{terminal}
grep -n -E '^(Update|Reminder):' meeting.txt
\end{terminal}

This selects lines 3 and 4. Without the parentheses,
\cmd+^Update|Reminder:+ means a line beginning with
\texttt{Update}, or a line containing \texttt{Reminder:} anywhere.
Moving a colon outside a group makes it apply after either
alternative. Test your expression against a deliberately bad
near-match, not only against the line you hope to find.
For simple independent phrases, repeat \cmd|-e| to supply more
than one pattern. The following example stays in fixed-string
mode and accepts a line containing either of the two phrases:

\begin{terminal}
grep -n -F -e 'gate opens' -e 'tea' meeting.txt errands.txt
\end{terminal}

There should be three lines: the update, the reminder and the
errand for tea bags. Each \cmd|-e| has its own pattern operand.
This spelling is also useful for a pattern that starts with a
hyphen, since \cmd|-e| makes explicit that its operand is the
pattern, not another option.

\begin{quicktable}{M{1.04in}Y}
\toprule
Switch & Use it when \ldots \\
\midrule
\texttt{-F} & You have literal text copied from a note or a message. \\
none & You only need basic regular expressions, such as a line-start anchor. \\
\texttt{-E} & You need alternatives, grouping or other extended syntax such as
  \texttt{+} for one or more repetitions. \\
\texttt{-c} & You need a count of matched lines per file, not a count of occurrences. \\
\texttt{-l} & You need the names of the files with matches, not the lines themselves. \\
\bottomrule
\end{quicktable}

\begin{dialoguebox}{The quotes did their job}
\Brian{I quoted the brackets, and I still got too many matches. What did I protect?}
\Stephen{You protected the brackets from the shell. I should have said which program the quotes affect.}
\Brian{Then the same brackets arrive intact at \texttt{grep}, which reads them its own way?}
\Stephen{Right. By default, brackets introduce a choice of characters.}
\Brian{But this note really has brackets. I don't want a choice.}
\Stephen{Add \texttt{-F}. Now the choice is made before the search: the pattern is literal.}
\Brian{So the quotes still belong there, even with \texttt{-F}?}
\Stephen{Yes. They get your literal pattern to the program in the first place.}
\DialogueConclusion{Quotes protect the argument; options set its meaning.}
\end{dialoguebox}

\section{Make silence explain itself}
Standard output alone is not enough to decide whether the search
worked. \cmd|grep| has three classes of exit status: zero means
one or more selected lines, 1 means no selected lines, and a
value greater than 1 means an error. In GNU grep, the usual
error status is 2. An error can happen after a match has already
been printed from an earlier file; visible output doesn't rule
out partial failure. Don't discard diagnostic output until you
understand what you are discarding.

\begin{terminal}
grep -F 'snow' meeting.txt
printf 'status: %s\n' "$?"
grep -F 'snow' absent.txt
printf 'status: %s\n' "$?"
\end{terminal}

\noindent
The first command prints no matching lines and its status is 1.
The second also prints no matching lines, but it should report
that the named file could not be opened, and its status is
greater than 1. Diagnostic wording varies. \cmd|$?| supplies the
status of the command that just finished; read it immediately.
Even \cmd|pwd| run in between would replace the value. Don't
use the quiet option \cmd|-q| as a complete file-read check:
a successful match may cause a zero status even if an error
also occurred. A pipeline such as \cmd+grep -F 'snow' meeting.txt | wc -l+
usually reports the status of the last command in a portable
shell, not \cmd|grep|'s, as in Chapter~\ref{ch:17}.

\begin{figure}[htbp]
\centering
\input{figures/chapter-19/status.tikz}
\caption{A search that found nothing is different from a search that could not run.}
\label{fig:19-status}
\VisualDescription{Three boxes distinguish match, no match and error by exit status even when standard output is empty or incomplete.}
\end{figure}

\section{A repeatable notes search}
Use a narrow list of files first. A name pattern on the command
line can extend the search to visible text notes once you have
checked which paths it expands to:

\begin{terminal}
printf '%s\n' ./*.txt
grep -l -i -F 'bring' ./*.txt
grep -n -i -F 'bring' ./*.txt
\end{terminal}

Both files should appear in the name-only output. In the last
command, look for gloves in the meeting file and tea bags in
the errands. The unquoted \cmd|./*.txt| is a shell glob, not a
\cmd|grep| pattern. The shell matches names before invoking the
program, and the \texttt{./} prefix keeps a leading hyphen in a
filename from looking like an option. This example does not
search subdirectories or hidden files. If the preview shows the
pattern unchanged or the shell complains it couldn't match
anything, check the working directory instead of assuming the
notes are empty. Files with a \texttt{.txt} suffix can still be
binary or have an encoding unsuitable for the current locale.

What about \cmd|grep -R|, often seen in recipes? Recursive
options and their rules for symbolic links are outside the
portable examples here. Qualify the tree carefully before using
them; a home directory can contain program caches, hidden
settings and mounts unrelated to the task. Searching explicitly
selected plain text is easier to check and less likely to
surface unrelated private information. Case folding and
character classes can depend on the locale, especially outside
ASCII. Accents are not necessarily ignored by \cmd|-i|, and
two visually identical words can be stored with different
Unicode sequences (Chapter~\ref{ch:18}). Try a distinctive
ASCII part of the phrase before drawing conclusions.

\begin{platformnote}{Linux, macOS and the BSDs}
The examples use options specified by POSIX and portable BRE or
ERE syntax, not GNU-only extensions such as \texttt{-P} for a
different regular expression language. GNU grep is common on
Linux, while macOS, FreeBSD, OpenBSD and NetBSD ship their own
base-system utilities. Check \texttt{man grep} on the machine
running the search, particularly for recursion, context lines,
binary inputs and color. Steps here were tested on Linux only.
\end{platformnote}

\begin{furtherreading}
\ReadingItem{POSIX grep}{Options and the distinction between an empty selection and an error}{https://pubs.opengroup.org/onlinepubs/9799919799/utilities/grep.html}
\ReadingItem{POSIX regular expressions}{Rules for basic and extended expressions and the effect of locales}{https://pubs.opengroup.org/onlinepubs/9799919799/basedefs/V1_chap09.html}
\end{furtherreading}

\begin{takeaways}
\item Use \texttt{-F} first for words you can remember or copy literally.
\item Know which parser sees each symbol: the shell handles the filename glob, then grep handles the content pattern.
\item Inspect the search scope, output and status before acting on the result.
\end{takeaways}

\begin{exercise}{Find out who is bringing what}
\label{ex:19-bring}
Search both files for \texttt{Bring}, showing filenames and line
numbers. Expect two lines, one from each file. This is a read-only
exercise with no files to clean up.
\end{exercise}
\begin{solution}
\begin{terminal}
grep -n -F 'Bring' meeting.txt errands.txt
\end{terminal}
Look for \texttt{meeting.txt:2} and \texttt{errands.txt:2}.
\end{solution}

\begin{exercise}{Pin down the whole line}
\label{ex:19-whole}
Select only a line that says exactly \texttt{Bring gloves}.
Expect one result. Repeat with just \texttt{Bring} as the
pattern and confirm that the shorter pattern selects nothing.
There is no new output file to remove.
\end{exercise}
\begin{solution}
\begin{terminal}
grep -F -x 'Bring gloves' meeting.txt
grep -F -x 'Bring' meeting.txt
printf 'status: %s\n' "$?"
\end{terminal}
The second search prints nothing; its status is 1.
\end{solution}

\begin{exercise}{Try an intentionally wrong anchor}
\label{ex:19-anchor}
Search for the label \texttt{Reminder:} at the start of a line.
Then temporarily move your anchor to the end of the pattern.
Expect one match in the first test and none in the second.
Both searches are read-only; use the chapter cleanup afterwards.
\end{exercise}
\begin{solution}
\begin{terminal}
grep -n '^Reminder:' meeting.txt
grep -n 'Reminder:$' meeting.txt
\end{terminal}
The first prints line 4. In the second, the reminder label
would have to end the line; instead, there is a message after it.
\end{solution}

\paragraph{Cleanup.}
Run \cmd|pwd| and compare it with \cmd+printf '%s\n' "$practice"+.
Only if both name the chapter's disposable directory, remove the
two practice files. \cmd|-i| asks before each removal; decline
anything you do not recognize. An unexpected leftover makes
\cmd|rmdir| fail harmlessly, so inspect it rather than switching
to recursive removal. If you started a separate \cmd|sh|, type
\cmd|exit| after cleanup to return to your usual shell.

\begin{terminal}
rm -i ./meeting.txt ./errands.txt
cd /tmp
rmdir "$practice"
\end{terminal}
